# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored (.env.example is the committed template).

# ── LiveKit Cloud (video/screen-share conference; C-RTC) ──────────────
# From your LiveKit Cloud project → Settings → Keys.
# LIVEKIT_URL is public (sent to the client). LIVEKIT_API_SECRET must stay
# server-side only — never bundle it into the client.
LIVEKIT_URL=wss://YOUR-PROJECT.livekit.cloud
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=YOUR_API_KEY
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=YOUR_API_SECRET

# ── Mumble voice (optional, suggestion only) ──────────────────────────
# The DESKTOP app is the Mumble client: it connects straight to the voice server
# from the user's machine and keeps its own name, password and certificate
# locally. The server never proxies Mumble and stores no credentials for it.
# These three are only a default address the desktop Settings panel offers, so
# users don't have to type it. The browser build has no Mumble support.
# MUMBLE_HOST=mumble.example.com
# MUMBLE_PORT=64738
# MUMBLE_CHANNEL=Lobby

# ── TimeTracking (optional, suggestion only) ──────────────────────────
# The DESKTOP app is the TimeTracking client, exactly like Mumble: it talks to
# the TimeTracking server straight from the user's machine and keeps the login
# there (address + username in Electron's userData, password in the OS
# keychain). This server never proxies TimeTracking and stores no credentials
# for it — it only receives the resulting one-word status ("working", "break",
# …) so a character can show it. The browser build has no TimeTracking support.
# This is only a default address the desktop app's settings offer, so users
# don't have to type it.
# TIMETRACKING_URL=https://timetracking.example.com

# ── Zitadel / OpenID Connect login (optional) ─────────────────────────
# A second way into an account, beside the login id + password. Configure an
# application in Zitadel of type "Web" with the code flow (PKCE is used either
# way) and paste its client id — and, for a confidential client, its secret.
# The redirect URI must be registered there EXACTLY as written here; it is never
# derived from a request Host.
#
# A first sign-in provisions the account: the login id is derived from
# preferred_username (the local part of a login name like alice@corp.example),
# and the immutable `sub` is what the account is keyed to, so a rename in the
# directory keeps the same avatar, agent token and position.
#
# With PIXEL_OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE set, the directory is authoritative for who is an
# admin: the role grants it and losing the role revokes it. Requires "Assert
# Roles on Authentication" (or the roles scope) on the Zitadel project, so the
# claim actually arrives. It can never revoke the LAST usable admin.
#
# The admin token stays the break-glass path — keep one set.
# PIXEL_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-instance.zitadel.cloud
# PIXEL_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=123456789@your_project
# PIXEL_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=                       # omit for a public (PKCE-only) client
# PIXEL_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://pixel.example.com/auth/oauth/callback
# PIXEL_OIDC_LABEL=Zitadel                        # what the sign-in button says
#
# The admin panel's Sign-in tab can override the three fields above (issuer, client
# id, redirect URI), the SCOPES below, and the label — and hide the button or send
# visitors straight to the provider. So a server with none of these set can be
# switched on from inside the app. Three consequences worth knowing:
#   • CLIENT_SECRET is never editable there, and it is WITHHELD from the flow as
#     soon as the issuer or client id is overridden — the secret belongs to the
#     client this file names, and must never be sent to another directory. An
#     overridden connection is a public client and needs PKCE (Zitadel: tick "PKCE"
#     / use a User Agent or Native app, or a Web app with no secret).
#   • Editing SCOPES changes which claims arrive. If you map an admin role below,
#     the scope that delivers the roles claim must stay in the list — without it the
#     next sign-in reports no roles, and admin is revoked from everyone but the last
#     usable one.
#   • PIXEL_OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE is editable there too, so pointing this at a directory
#     whose role is simply called "admin" needs no redeploy.
#   • The rest stays environment-only: which CLAIM carries the roles, and whose
#     existing account a directory username may take over.
# PIXEL_OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email          # "openid" is added if you leave it out
# PIXEL_OIDC_ADMIN_ROLE=pixel-admin               # unset → the local admin flag is left alone
#                                                 #   Roles are read from the ID token AND from
#                                                 #   userinfo, under this claim and Zitadel's
#                                                 #   project-scoped variants — so "Assert Roles on
#                                                 #   Authentication" alone is enough. Each sign-in
#                                                 #   logs the roles it saw.
# PIXEL_OIDC_ROLES_CLAIM=urn:zitadel:iam:org:project:roles
# PIXEL_OIDC_CLAIM_EXISTING=1                     # 0 → never adopt an existing local account
# PIXEL_OIDC_END_SESSION=0                        # 1 → /logout also ends the session at Zitadel
#                                                 #     (register the post-logout URI there)

# ── TLS (optional override) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The server serves HTTPS when cert.pem + key.pem exist in the data dir
# (alongside the SQLite DB; default: tmp/data in the repo, where a dev
# certificate is generated on first start). Override the paths here,
# e.g. later for Let's Encrypt:
# PIXEL_TLS_CERT=/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem
# PIXEL_TLS_KEY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem
